  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Wed Jul 1 2026\n\n**** Hot and Humid Weather Continues ****\n\nWe have extended the Extreme Heat Warning until 7 pm Friday \nfrom Schuyler, Cass, Menard, Logan, DeWitt, Piatt, Champaign and\nVermilion counties south, while areas north of there have the \nExtreme Heat Warning going through midnight Thu night. There is \nsome variables with how far south and extent of convection will \nbe over central IL on Friday afternoon especially northern \ncounties. This area may very well need heat headlines extended \ninto Friday too if convection stays further north. \n\nHeat Indices ranged from 98-110F across CWA at 2 pm with the\nhighest numbers generally from highway 51 east. Temperatures \nwere in the upper 80s/lower 90s with dewpoints in the mid to \nupper 70s. SSW winds were 7-15 mph with few gusts to 20 mph. Few\nto scattered cumulus clouds se of the IL river with the cu \nfield more prominent in eastern IL. We should remain in this\ntropical airmass rest of this week, and similar heat indices\nexpected to peak from 100-110F Thursday afternoon/early Thu\nevening. Heat indices around 105F or higher still expected from\nI-72 south on Fri afternoon with 100-105 north of I-72.  \n\nWe kept a dry forecast going through Thu night over much of CWA\nexcept slight pops far sw CWA during mid/late Thu afternoon from\nTaylorville and Effingham sw. Convection chances over northern\nIL Thu night could spill down into northern CWA overnight Thu \nnight into Fri morning. Ouflow boundary from this nighttime\nconvection to likely redevelop convection in unstable airmass\nover parts of central IL Friday afternoon. Question is how far\nsouth this convection gets and stayed close to NBM pops for now\nwhich have increase from previous runs.  \n\n\n**** Thunderstorms for the Holiday Weekend ****\n\nNBM has increase chances of convection over central IL on Friday\nespecially northern cwa by Fri afternoon with 50-60% pops. SPC\nDay3 outlook has marginal risk of severe storms Friday\nafternoon/evening for all of CWA with CIG1 of stronger storms\npossible over Iowa. Primarily for damaging winds and heavy \nrains in central and southeast IL. SPC Day 4-5 for this weekend\ndoes not have any severe wx outlooks for IL, but the CSU-MLP \nand CIPS shows some at least some marginal risk of severe over \nmuch of CWA Saturday and Sunday as unstable air mass lingers. \nWPC Day2-3 ERO for Thu night thru Fri night primarily has \nslight to marginal risk of excessive rainfall north of CWA from \nI-80 north. WPC Day4-5 ERO for Sat into Sunday night has \nmarginal risk of excessive rainfall over much of IL including \nour CWA. \n\nThe upper level ridge will shift further west across the Rockies\nand Desert Southwest, with some weak trofing over the Midwest\nincluding IL by mid week. This will bring a slight cooling \ntrend next work week with temperatures trending closer to normal\nfor early July with highs in the upper 80s to near 90F and lows\nin the upper 60s to near 70F. We have a daily chance (20-40%)\nMonday through Wed especially during the afternoon and early\nevening hours.  \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for July\n8-14th has 33-40% chance of above normal temperatures over\ncentral/se IL with precipitation trending near normal.",
